# OCR PROJECT REPORT (CRNN + CTC)

## Objective
The goal of this project is to build an Optical Character Recognition (OCR) system using a deep learning approach that converts image-based text into machine-readable character sequences. The system is designed to handle noisy, distorted, and variable-length text images using a CRNN architecture combined with CTC loss.

## Dataset Description
The dataset consists of grayscale images of text along with a CSV file mapping each image to its corresponding label. The dataset is divided into training images, training labels, and test images. The character set includes uppercase English alphabets (A–Z) and digits (0–9), making a total of 36 classes.

## Data Preprocessing
All images are converted to grayscale and resized to 32×128 pixels. Pixel values are normalized to stabilize training. Data augmentation techniques such as small random rotations and affine transformations are applied to improve robustness against distortions, noise, and skewed text.

## Label Encoding
Each character is mapped to an integer index where A–Z are mapped to 1–26 and digits 0–9 are mapped to 27–36. The blank token required for CTC loss is assigned index 0. Text labels are converted into sequences of integers for training.

## Model Architecture
The model used is a CRNN (Convolutional Recurrent Neural Network). The CNN component extracts spatial features from images using multiple convolutional layers with batch normalization and ReLU activation. Max pooling layers reduce spatial dimensions. The extracted features are then passed into a bidirectional LSTM which captures sequential dependencies. Finally, a fully connected layer maps the output to character probabilities.

## Loss Function
CTC (Connectionist Temporal Classification) loss is used because it allows training without requiring explicit alignment between input images and output text sequences. It is well-suited for OCR tasks where character boundaries are unknown.

## Decoding Strategy
Two decoding strategies are used. Greedy decoding selects the most probable character at each timestep. Beam search decoding improves performance by exploring multiple possible character sequences and selecting the most likely one, reducing prediction errors.

## Evaluation Metric
Character Error Rate (CER) is used as the evaluation metric. It is calculated using Levenshtein distance between predicted and true text divided by the number of characters in the ground truth.

## Training Setup
The model is trained using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.001. Batch size is set to 16 and training runs for multiple epochs. The best model is saved based on validation CER performance. Training is performed on GPU when available or CPU otherwise.

## Training Pipeline
The dataset is split into training and validation sets. Data is loaded using PyTorch DataLoader with a custom collate function to handle variable-length sequences. The model is trained iteratively, computing loss and updating weights using backpropagation.

## Results
The model successfully learns to recognize text from images and shows decreasing Character Error Rate over epochs. It performs well on noisy and distorted images, demonstrating strong generalization ability.

## Error Analysis
Errors mainly occur in cases of blurred characters, overlapping symbols, and low-quality images. A qualitative comparison between predicted and ground truth text is performed to analyze failure cases.

## Test Prediction
The trained model is used to generate predictions on unseen test images. Beam search decoding is applied to improve output quality. The final predictions are saved in a CSV file named submission.csv in the required format of image and predicted text.

## Conclusion
This project demonstrates an effective OCR system using CRNN with CTC loss. The combination of CNN for feature extraction, BiLSTM for sequence modeling, and beam search decoding results in a robust system capable of handling real-world noisy text recognition tasks.

## Deliverables
The final submission includes the trained model file (best.pth), prediction file (submission.csv), notebook code, report, and optional demo images or video.

